WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.571
Sending a file the obvious way passes every byte through your process twice.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.585
The kernel keeps recently used file pages in memory, in its page cache.

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.240
Your loop calls read, and the kernel copies the bytes up into your buffer.

4
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.560
That's one copy, and one system call: a switch into the kernel and back.

5
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:27.155
Then it calls write, and the kernel copies them back down into the socket's send buffer.

6
00:00:27.155 --> 00:00:29.604
A second copy, and a second system call.

7
00:00:29.604 --> 00:00:32.176
Only then does the network card send them.

8
00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:37.493
A heap ByteBuffer adds one more copy per call, through a temporary direct buffer.

9
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:44.213
And 64 MiB through a 64 KiB buffer is 1,024 rounds of this.

10
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:48.620
FileChannel.transferTo asks the kernel to do the whole move.

11
00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:53.364
On Linux, with a socket as the target, it becomes one sendfile call.

12
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:57.766
The kernel sends pages from the page cache straight to the socket.

13
00:00:57.766 --> 00:00:59.993
The bytes never enter your process.

14
00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:01.075
That's zero-copy.

15
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:06.953
Counted with strace, transferTo made one sendfile call.

16
00:01:06.953 --> 00:01:10.956
The copy made 2,119 reads and 1,037 writes.

17
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:14.901
transferTo may move fewer bytes than you asked for.

18
00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:19.531
It returns how many it moved, so call it in a loop, in Java or in Kotlin.

19
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:24.996
One condition: the bytes must leave exactly as they sit in the file.

20
00:01:24.996 --> 00:01:29.176
TLS or compression inside the JVM rules zero-copy out.

21
00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:31.606
Now, who does the read?

22
00:01:31.606 --> 00:01:35.313
epoll answers one question: which sockets are ready?

23
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:39.871
Then your loop makes the read call itself, one more call per ready socket.

24
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:42.055
io_uring works by completion.

25
00:01:42.055 --> 00:01:46.099
Your program and the kernel share two ring buffers in memory.

26
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:51.911
You put a request on the submission ring: read up to 16 KiB from this socket.

27
00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:58.324
The kernel does the read, and puts the result on the completion ring: done, 4,096 bytes.

28
00:01:58.533 --> 00:02:04.193
One system call can submit and collect many operations, and the kernel moves the bytes.

29
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.067
The JDK's selector and poller use epoll.

30
00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:12.053
io_uring comes through Netty, first-class since 4.2.

31
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:13.238
Is it faster?

32
00:02:13.238 --> 00:02:14.242
Not reliably.

33
00:02:14.242 --> 00:02:19.651
In the lesson's demo, epoll took 18 milliseconds and io_uring took 65.

34
00:02:19.651 --> 00:02:21.891
So measure your own workload.

35
00:02:21.891 --> 00:02:25.600
The switch is one factory and one channel class.
